## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic data structures with tight amortized bounds in a streaming context, including handling duplicates and edge cases like K=0. It also probes practical trade-offs for sliding windows.

## Key Concepts
- Maintain counts and a running pair counter
- Special-case K=0 to avoid double counting
- Support batched inserts/removals within a fixed window

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowPairCounter {
  constructor(K) {
    this.K = K; this.cnt = new Map(); this.pairs = 0;
  }
  add(x) {
    const k = this.K;
    if (k === 0) this.pairs += (this.cnt.get(x) || 0);
    else {
      const a = this.cnt.get(x + k) || 0;
      const b = this.cnt.get(x - k) || 0;
      this.pairs += a + b;
    }
    this.cnt.set(x, (this.cnt.get(x) || 0) + 1);
  }
  remove(x) {
    const k = this.K;
    const cur = this.cnt.get(x) || 0;
    if (k === 0) this.pairs -= (cur - 1);
    else {
      const a = this.cnt.get(x + k) || 0;
      const b = this.cnt.get(x - k) || 0;
      this.pairs -= (a + b);
    }
    if (cur === 1) this.cnt.delete(x); else this.cnt.set(x, cur - 1);
  }
  batch(addArr, removeArr) {
    for (const x of removeArr) this.remove(x);
    for (const x of addArr) this.add(x);
  }
  exists() { return this.pairs > 0; }
}
```

